 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Soy, sugar and sesame oil in the rice brown faster and deeper than plain rice, so the crust gets real colour at medium-low heat — and every bite is seasoned, not just the outside.</li><li>(This is Just One Cookbook’s method.)</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Why season the rice"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Rice sticks and tears on a bare pan; on parchment the crust forms and lifts off cleanly.</li><li>A good non-stick pan works without it.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Why parchment"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Just One Cookbook suggests unagi or teriyaki sauce instead of soy.</li><li>Over charcoal or under a broiler works too — watch it closely.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Other glazes"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>The miso glaze is Just One Cookbook’s miso yaki onigiri (she brushes it on plain salted rice).</li><li>The butter on top is Chopstick Chronicles’ soy-butter version.</li><li>Putting both on one page, and the butter on JOC’s seasoned rice, is this site’s choice.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Variations: where they’re from"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Rice hardens in the fridge.</li><li>If you must, wrap them and then a thick towel, up to 2 days; let them come back to room temperature and reheat in a pan.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Fridge"
  }
 ],
